We have been talking about the fuzzy "balanced" term. And the claim that
Nethack is well balanced is only false in a very restricted definition
of "balanced". Clearly folks here have different associations about what
makes a game well balanced or badly balanced. (Having different fundaments
what one considers balanced and claiming to have the one true definition
is nothing to sanely base a discussion on.) The existance of objects with
distinguished grades of usefulness make a game primarily non-boring, not
necessarily unbalanced. Balance gets important if you abstract from the
induvidual objects and take into account the relations of all objects and
game mechanics, including the random degrees. That does not mean that a
game couldn't get unbalanced by an *overly* powerful universal object;
but this is not the case with the game of Nethack. This is my opinion.

